      <section>
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <section>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Here is a different type of inequality: \(x^2\leq4\).</para>

      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Which of the following values solve the inequality: 1, 3 or -3?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>From the given values, only 1 is a solution of the inequality.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Describe all solutions to this inequality. (If you like, you can graph the solutions
            on a number line.)</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( -2 ≤ x ≤ 2 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Describe all solutions to the inequality \(x^2\geq9\). Test several numbers to make
            sure your answer is correct.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( x ≤-3 \) or \( x ≥ 3 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
      <!-- END ARE YOU READY FOR MORE-->
    </section>
    </section>

    <section>
      <note class="self-check">
        <para>Which number line represents the solution to the
        inequality?\(\frac12(–2z\;+\;2)\;\geq\;12\)</para>
      </note>
